::GEO_U06_L07_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.11
Question:
A plane slices a rectangular prism parallel to its base. What cross-section is expected?
{
=A. Rectangle#The slice parallel to a rectangular base forms a rectangle.
~B. Circle#A rectangular prism does not have circular bases.
~C. Parabola#A parabola is a conic section.
~D. Sphere#A cross-section is two-dimensional, not a sphere.
}

::GEO_U06_L07_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.12
Question:
Which solid is formed by rotating a right triangle around one leg?
{
~A. Cylinder#A cylinder comes from rotating a rectangle.
=B. Cone#The other leg sweeps out the radius of a cone.
~C. Sphere#A sphere comes from rotating a semicircle.
~D. Cube#A cube is not created by this rotation.
}

::GEO_U06_L07_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L07_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.16
Question:
Which shape is a conic section?
{
~A. Cube#A cube is a solid, not a conic section.
~B. Cylinder#A cylinder is a solid, not a conic section.
=C. Ellipse#An ellipse can be formed by slicing a cone.
~D. Rectangular prism#A rectangular prism is a solid, not a conic section.
}

::GEO_U06_L07_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L07_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.11
Question:
What determines the cross-section of a solid?
{
~A. Only the color of the diagram#Color is not geometric evidence.
~B. Only the name of the student drawing it#This is unrelated to Geometry.
~C. Only the size of the page#Page size does not determine a cross-section.
=D. The solid and the slicing plane#Both the solid and slicing direction determine the cross-section.
}

::GEO_U06_L07_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L07_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.12
Question:
Rotating a circle around a diameter creates which solid?
{
=A. Sphere#A circle rotated around a diameter sweeps out a sphere.
~B. Cone#A cone comes from rotating a right triangle.
~C. Cylinder#A cylinder comes from rotating a rectangle.
~D. Pyramid#A pyramid is not a solid of rotation from a circle.
}

